
  First clarifying question.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the stored ID identifies [3mthe browser[0m or [3mthe person[0m. A browser-scoped ID is written once and reused
  for every login attempt from that browser forever — which means logins from two different accounts on the same machine
  carry the same ID, and anything consuming that ID can link those accounts together. An account-scoped ID resets when
  the user changes, so it can't link accounts, but then it isn't really "persistent across page loads" until after a
  successful login tells you who the user is — there's a bootstrapping gap on the very first attempt, which is exactly
  the attempt you most want to trace.

  - [1mBrowser-scoped[0m: simplest, always available, traces every attempt including failed and first-ever ones. Cost: it is a
    cross-account linking identifier, and that is the part that needs a deliberate yes.
  - [1mAccount-scoped[0m: no cross-account linkage, but unavailable on the first attempt and on failed attempts from a fresh
    browser — the gap swallows the main use case.
  - [1mBrowser-scoped with a reset[0m: same as the first, plus an explicit clear (on logout, or a reset call) so the
    identifier isn't unbounded in time. Keeps failed-attempt tracing, caps the linking window.

  My recommendation is [1mbrowser-scoped with a reset on logout[0m. It keeps the property you're actually buying — tracing an
  attempt before you know who the user is — while bounding how long one identifier accumulates history. Note there's no
  logout in the app today, so "reset" would mean adding a small clear function now and calling it from a logout when one
  exists.
